<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmanas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brahmanas are Vedic prose texts, composed roughly between 900 and 700 BCE, that explain the meaning and correct performance of the sacrificial rituals of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. Attached to particular Vedic schools, they include the Aitareya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmanas</a>, and they contain myths, accounts of kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janamejaya">Janamejaya</a> and early speculation that leads into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>. They are a key source for the society of the Kuru and Panchala realms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brahmanas.jpg" alt="Brahmanas" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1636_CE_Samaveda,_Sadvimsha_Brahmana_(Pa%C3%B1cavi%E1%B9%83%C5%9Babr%C4%81hma%E1%B9%87a_supplement),_Benares_Sanskrit_college,_Edward_Cowell_collection,_sample_i,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Brahmanas are Vedic works attached to the Samhitas, the hymns and mantras, of the Rig, Sama, Yajur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharva Vedas</a>. Each Veda has one or more Brahmanas of its own, and each is generally associated with a particular shakha, or Vedic school. The word is loosely translated as explanations of sacred knowledge or doctrine, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayana">Sayana</a> a Brahmana has two parts: vidhi, directions for rites, and arthavada, explanatory remarks. The oldest is dated to about 900 BCE and the most recent to around 700 BCE, though the dating is controversial, since the texts were probably recorded only after several centuries of oral transmission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Brahmanas are noted for their instructions on the proper performance of rituals and for explaining the symbolism of sacred words and ritual actions. They insist on exact pronunciation, metre and pitch, with coordinated movement of the hands and fingers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a>, for example, states that verbal perfection made a mantra infallible, while one mistake made it powerless. Scholars suggest that this care preserved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> in an age without writing, when students memorised them and passed them on by recitation.</p><p>The texts also record legends and discuss scientific matters of the Vedic period, including observational astronomy and, in relation to altar construction, geometry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> has the Aitareya and Kausitaki Brahmanas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a> the Panchavimsha, or Tandya, Brahmana, which includes the Vratyastoma, a ceremony by which people of non-Aryan stock could be admitted into the Aryan family. The Shatapatha belongs to the White Yajurveda, the Taittiriya to the Black Yajurveda, and the Gopatha to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>. Some Brahmanas also contain mystical and philosophical material that forms the Aranyakas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Fewer than twenty Brahmanas survive. Most are lost, and the grammarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patanjali">Patanjali</a>'s Mahabhasya imply that each Veda once had a much larger number. Panini also distinguishes old Brahmanas, taught before Krishna Dvaipayana Vyasa set out the Vedic recensions, from new ones expounded by his disciples. The grammarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaska">Yaska</a>'s Nirukta, a work on the etymology and interpretation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, refers to several of them.</p><p>In the 14th century, King Bukka asked his preceptor and minister Madhavacharya to write a commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> so that common people could understand the mantras. Madhavacharya entrusted the task to his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayana">Sayana</a>, who wrote commentaries on the Samhitas, Brahmanas, Aranyakas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brahmana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shruti, “what is heard”, is revealed knowledge received by the ancient seers: the four Vedas, the Rigveda, Samaveda, Yajurveda and Atharvaveda, each with its <b>Brahmanas</b>, ritual commentaries; Aranyakas, forest treatises; and Upanishads, philosophical dialogues.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The later Vedic texts, the <b>Brahmanas</b>, which explain the rituals, and the Upanishads, which explore the nature of the self and ultimate reality, show the language changing towards its classical form.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The four Vedas are its core, with the <b>Brahmanas</b>, the Aranyakas, the older Upanishads and the oldest Śrautasutras.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>In the wider sense the name Rigveda also covers the <b>Brahmanas</b>, Aranyakas and Upanishads handed down with the Samhita.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The mukhya Upanishads are found mostly in the concluding part of the <b>Brahmanas</b> and Aranyakas.</blockquote><a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The verse is cited throughout Hindu literature: in the <b>Brahmanas</b> and Srauta-sutras of the Vedic liturgy, in Upanishads such as the Brihadaranyaka, Shvetashvatara and Maitrayaniya, which treat it esoterically, and in the Mahabharata, the Harivamsa and the Manusmṛti.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gayatri-mantra">Gayatri Mantra</a></li></ul></section>
